Fillets: the start of a rounding along a knife edge, where two faces meet
tangentially, at 0 degrees, the material between them a wedge that thins
to nothing (mitcad#1984).

A ball of the radius touches both faces only where the wedge is two radii
thick, away from the edge. The walked fillet's first section was solved
from the edge's point on both faces, where the ball's equations have no
solution near (the centres a ball touching each face would have lie on
opposite sides of the edge): no start, for two cylinders tangent to each
other at any radius (a plane and a cylinder with parallel axes are
computed analytically, ChFiKPart, which finds the ball).

- ChFi3d_KnifeStart, from StartSol for a constant rounding at each point it
  tries on the spine, before PerformFirstSection: where the two surfaces'
  centre sides (their normals with the signs Choix gives them, as
  BlendFunc_ConstRad::Set has them) are opposite within a thousandth of a
  radian at the edge's point, it steps from there along face 1 into the
  face, square to the edge in the section plane, keeping the centre of a
  ball touching face 1 and its foot on surface 2, until the centre's
  distance from surface 2 on that surface's centre side is the radius; the
  points there are the solver's start. The walk, the ends and the topology
  are OCCT's as for any rounding.
- ChFi3d::ConcaveSide, where the faces turn back on each other at the
  edge (the branch a knife edge takes): the side of the section (Choix's
  parity, from the normals' cross product along the edge) is taken with
  the normals turned towards the centre, as in the other branch; with one
  face's normal turned and not the other's, the section went the other way
  round the ball, and the fillet gave the tip and the whole ball's section
  as the body (two cylinders whose seams lie on the edge, as a sketch's
  circles have them).
- Where face 1 or 2 ends before the ball fits (the radius larger than the
  wedge allows), or the faces do not meet as a knife, the start is the
  edge's point as before.

